  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Patchy dense fog this morning in Northwest Montana, improving \n   by late morning.\n\n-  Seasonably mild and showery conditions continue through Monday\n   with generally light precipitation and snow levels above \n   valley floors.\n\n-  A colder system Tuesday through Thursday brings plowable snow\n   to mountains and potentially in valleys east of US-93 with a \n   rain/snow mix to all other valley locations.\n\nWeakening thermal inversions remain established across the region\nthis morning with cooling aloft over the past 24 hours. Mid and \nhigh- level cloud cover streaming in from the southwest continues \nto inhibit efficient radiational cooling in valley bottoms south \nof the I-90 corridor, limiting widespread dense fog formation in \nlocations like Missoula and Hamilton. However, dense fog remains \nentrenched in the Flathead and lower Clark Fork valleys. \nAdditionally, localized clearing will continue to allow for \ntemperature and dewpoint depressions to remain close, resulting in\nperiodic development of patchy mist and low stratus through mid-\nmorning.\n\nA progression of weak troughs embedded in southwesterly flow will\nbring widely scattered light showers to the region today through \nMonday. Precipitation amounts will remain light, generally ranging\nfrom a few hundredths up to 0.20 inches in the valleys. Snow \nlevels will remain elevated between 5,000 to 6,000 feet through \nthe weekend. Light southwesterly winds will continue, with the \nstrongest gusts focused along mountain passes, in higher terrain, \nand north-south oriented valleys and gaps.\n\nA stronger thermal gradient and transient surface low will track\nthrough the souther half of the region Tuesday into Thursday, \nintroducing a colder airmass. Snow levels will become highly \ndiurnal, dropping to near 3,000 feet or lower during overnight \nperiods. Precipitation will generally fall as predominantly rain \nor a rain/snow mix in the valleys along and west of I-90 during \nthe daytime periods, possibly transitioning to light snow showers \novernight. \n\nStorm total precipitation amounts (liquid and melted snow) \nthrough Thursday could reach as high as 1 to 1.5 inches in valley\nlocations favoring the southern track of the surface low south of\nFlathead Lake and along/east of US-93. For the higher terrain, \nincluding the highest peaks of the Bitterroots, southern Idaho \nCounty mountains, Lemhi County mountains, and mountains east of \nUS-93, high- end snowfall totals of 10 to 12 inches are possible \nthrough Thursday evening. Valley locations east of US-93 could see\na couple inches of wet, dense snow and periods of impacts to road\ntravel. While valley floor snow accumulations will be minimal \nwest of US-93, any overnight snowfall will likely melt during the\nday with the potential to re-freeze into patchy ice on elevated \nroadways during subsequent overnight periods.",
